Write bytecode for a machine of the other byte order (JS_WRITE_OBJ_BSWAP).

QuickJS-ng's bytecode serialization is in the byte order of the machine:
JS_WriteObject copies 16, 32 and 64 bit words and the function bytecode
as they are in memory, and JS_ReadObject reads them the same way. The
JS_WRITE_OBJ_BSWAP flag is defined as 0 ("handled transparently"), but
nothing swaps. Aura compiles its JavaScript prelude at build time on a
little-endian host for big-endian PowerPC, and the cross build must not
run PowerPC code, so the writer gets the flag back:

- 16, 32 and 64 bit fields are written swapped;
- function bytecode operands are swapped by opcode format (bc_byte_swap),
  with each format's operand widths checked against the opcode size;
- the compiled form of a regular expression literal, a constant pool
  string holding libregexp bytecode, is found through the OP_regexp that
  uses it and written through lre_byte_swap (libregexp.c), which names
  every opcode so a new one fails instead of going out unswapped;
- the checksum is the one the reading machine computes;
- typed arrays are refused.

The reader is unchanged. Output written with the flag can only be read
on a machine of the other byte order.

